    "subjectId": "gk-geography",
    "title": "Major Physiographic Divisions: Northern Mountains, Great Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Coastal Plains & Islands",
    "subtitle": "Geomorphological Structure, Gondwanaland Relict, Indo-Gangetic Alluvial Trough & Coastal Morphologies",
    "learningObjective": "Analyze the structural, geomorphological, and relief characteristics of India's five major physiographic divisions.",
    "introduction": "The Indian landmass presents extraordinary physiographic diversity, resulting from continental drift and the collision of the Indian Plate with the Eurasian Plate c. 50 million years ago. Geomorphologically, India is divided into five distinct macro-regions: the young fold mountains of the Northern Himalayas, the vast fertile Indo-Gangetic-Brahmaputra alluvial trough, the ancient stable Gondwana Peninsular Plateau, the maritime Coastal Plains, and the Oceanic Islands.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. The Five Macro Physiographic Divisions</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Northern Mountain Wall (The Himalayas):</strong> Youngest, loftiest tertiary fold mountains on earth, stretching in an eastward convex arc of 2,400 km from the Indus gorge to the Brahmaputra gorge. Width varies from 400 km in Kashmir to 150 km in Arunachal Pradesh.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Great Northern Plains (Indo-Gangetic-Brahmaputra Trough):</strong> A vast aggradational foredeep depression created during Himalayan orogeny and filled with fluvial sediment deposits brought by the Indus, Ganga, and Brahmaputra. Extends over 3,200 km with a width of 150\u2013300 km and sediment depths exceeding 2,000 meters. Sub-divided morphologically into <strong>Bhabhar, Terai, Bhangar, and Khadar</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Peninsular Plateau:</strong> The oldest, most rigid and stable geological shield of India, formed of ancient Archean gneisses, granites, and Gondwana sediments. Triangular in shape, flanked by the Aravallis in the northwest, Rajmahal hills in the northeast, Western Ghats (Sahyadris) in the west, and Eastern Ghats in the east. Divided into the Central Highlands (Malwa, Bundelkhand) and Deccan Plateau (covered by Cretaceous basalt lava traps).</li>\n  <li><strong>The Coastal Plains:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Western Coastal Plain:</em> Narrow, submerged coastline along the Arabian Sea; divided into Kathiawar, Konkan (Maharashtra/Goa), Kannad (Karnataka), and Malabar (Kerala, famous for lagoons or Kayals like Vembanad).</li>\n      <li><em>Eastern Coastal Plain:</em> Broad, emergent coastline along the Bay of Bengal; formed by colossal deltas of Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, and Cauvery; divided into Utkal coast, Northern Circars, and Coromandel coast (Tamil Nadu).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Oceanic Islands:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Andaman and Nicobar Islands:</em> Submerged peaks of the tertiary Arakan Yoma mountain chain in the Bay of Bengal; <strong>Ten Degree Channel</strong> separates Andaman from Nicobar. <strong>Barren Island</strong> is India's only active volcano.</li>\n      <li><em>Lakshadweep Islands:</em> 36 coral islands formed by coral polyps (atolls and fringing reefs) in the Arabian Sea; <strong>Nine Degree Channel</strong> separates Minicoy from the main Lakshadweep group.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>",
