    "title": "Koeppen's Climate Classification of India & Annual Seasons (Summer Loo, Retreating Monsoon)",
    "subtitle": "Koeppen Scheme (Amw, As, Aw, Cwg, BWhw), Kalbaisakhi, Mango Showers & Retreating Monsoon Oct-Nov",
    "learningObjective": "Master Koeppen's climatic classification of India, regional climatic types, and the four traditional meteorological seasons.",
    "introduction": "The India Meteorological Department (IMD) designates four official annual seasons: (1) Cold Weather Season (Winter: Dec\u2013Feb); (2) Hot Weather Season (Pre-Monsoon/Summer: March\u2013May); (3) Southwest Monsoon Season (Rainy: June\u2013Sept); and (4) Season of Retreating Monsoon (Post-Monsoon/Autumn: Oct\u2013Nov). Under <strong>Wladimir Koeppen's climatic classification</strong> (based on monthly temperature and precipitation thresholds), India features distinct macro-climatic regimes.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Koeppen's Climatic Classification of India</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cwg (Monsoon type with dry winter):</strong> Covers the entire <strong>Indo-Gangetic Plain, including all of Uttar Pradesh</strong>, northern MP, Bihar, and Assam. Characterized by hot summers (temp > 40\u00b0C), cool dry winters, and maximum rainfall during the summer monsoon.</li>\n  <li><strong>Amw (Monsoon type with short dry winter):</strong> Covers the coastal strip of western India (Konkan, Malabar coast, south of Mumbai). Receives >250 cm rainfall in summer.</li>\n  <li><strong>As (Monsoon type with dry summer):</strong> Covers the <strong>Coromandel Coast (Tamil Nadu and coastal Andhra)</strong>. Receives major rainfall during winter (Oct\u2013Dec) from the Retreating / Northeast Monsoon; summers are relatively dry.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aw (Tropical Savanna type):</strong> Covers the bulk of the Peninsular plateau south of the Tropic of Cancer.</li>\n  <li><strong>BShw (Semi-arid Steppe type):</strong> Covers rain-shadow belts of Western Ghats and semi-arid tracts of western Rajasthan, Haryana, and Gujarat.</li>\n  <li><strong>BWhw (Hot Desert type):</strong> Covers extreme western Rajasthan (Thar Desert: Jaisalmer, Bikaner) with rainfall < 20 cm.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dfc (Cold humid winter with short summer):</strong> Covers Arunachal Pradesh and Sikkim.</li>\n  <li><strong>E (Polar / Tundra type):</strong> Covers high-altitude mountain zones of Ladakh, J&K, and Himachal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Distinctive Pre-Monsoon & Autumn Weather Phenomena</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Loo (Summer Heatwave):</strong> Fierce, dry, scorching, dust-laden westerly winds blowing across Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan, and <strong>Uttar Pradesh</strong> during May and June, pushing afternoon temperatures above 45\u00b0C.</li>\n  <li><strong>Andhi:</strong> Violent evening dust-storms in UP and Delhi that temporarily lower temperatures.</li>\n  <li><strong>Kalbaisakhi (Nor'westers):</strong> Severe localized thunderstorms with violent gales and torrential rain in <strong>West Bengal</strong> and Assam during Baisakh (April\u2013May); beneficial for early tea and jute crops.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mango Showers & Cherry Blossoms:</strong> Pre-monsoon showers in Kerala and Karnataka; aids early ripening of mangoes and blooming of coffee blossoms.</li>\n  <li><strong>October Heat (Retreating Monsoon):</strong> Transition in October when skies clear, temperature rises, and oppressive humidity creates stifling weather, before winter arrives.</li>\n</ul>",
